       <div class="sect5">
+       <h6 id="pveceph_cephx_disabled">
+        Clusters with Cephx Disabled
+        <a class="headerlink" href="#pveceph_cephx_disabled" title="Permalink to this heading">
+        </a>
+       </h6>
+       <div class="paragraph">
+        <p>
+         Ceph evaluates these health checks from the stored keys and cipher settings,
+also when Cephx is disabled, that is, when
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          auth_cluster_required
+         </span>
+         ,
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          auth_service_required
+         </span>
+         , and
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          auth_client_required
+         </span>
+         are set to
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          none
+         </span>
+         .
+Disabling Cephx therefore does not clear them, and changing the authentication
+method interrupts existing clients, which need a restart or remount afterwards.
+        </p>
+       </div>
+       <div class="paragraph">
+        <p>
+         On a cluster with Cephx disabled:
+        </p>
+       </div>
+       <div class="olist arabic">
+        <ol class="arabic">
+         <li>
+          <p>
+           Run the helper without options and apply its plan. It migrates the manager,
+  metadata server, and OSD keys, restarting services one at a time when needed,
+  and switches the service tickets to the new cipher. This clears both
+  error-severity checks. If an old
+           <span class="monospaced">
+            mon.
+           </span>
+           key is stored in the authentication
+  database, the helper instead refuses and asks you to migrate that key first.
+          </p>
+         </li>
+         <li>
+          <p>
+           Optionally, migrate the bootstrap and crash keys with
+           <span class="monospaced">
+            --rotate-client-keys
+           </span>
+           .
+          </p>
+         </li>
+         <li>
+          <p>
+           Mute the remaining warnings without a duration. While Cephx is disabled, the
+  helper does not migrate the keys of other Ceph users, as their clients do not
+  authenticate with Cephx:
+          </p>
+          <div class="listingblock">
+           <div class="content">
+            <!-- Generator: GNU source-highlight
+by Lorenzo Bettini
+http://www.lorenzobettini.it
+http://www.gnu.org/software/src-highlite -->
+            <pre><tt>ceph health mute AUTH_INSECURE_CLIENT_KEY_TYPE --sticky
+ceph health mute AUTH_INSECURE_KEYS_ALLOWED --sticky
+ceph health mute AUTH_INSECURE_KEYS_CREATABLE --sticky</tt></pre>
+           </div>
+          </div>
+         </li>
+        </ol>
+       </div>
+       <div class="paragraph">
+        <p>
+         Do not restrict the allowed ciphers or forbid creating old-cipher keys by hand
+while the preferred cipher is still
+         <span class="monospaced">
+          aes
+         </span>
+         , as creating keys then fails.
+        </p>
+       </div>
+       <div class="paragraph">
+        <p>
+         Before you enable Cephx again, unmute these checks and make sure that every
+monitor, Ceph service, and client still has a key file matching the
+authentication database. Restart the monitors first, then all other Ceph
+services, and restart or remount the clients. Then run the migration as described in
+         <a href="#pveceph_cephx_migration_helper">
+          Migration Steps
+         </a>
+         .
+        </p>
+       </div>
